.TH GPU\-VACATE 1 2026-11-10 "gpu-vacate 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation (it splits file names) and no justification
.nh
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
gpu\-vacate \- move the GNOME desktop off the discrete GPU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpu\-vacate
.RB [ status ]
.br
.B gpu\-vacate
.B on
.RB [ \-\-keep ]
.br
.B gpu\-vacate
.B off
.br
.B gpu\-vacate
.BR inputs " | " sync " | " version " | " help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpu\-vacate
moves the GNOME desktop from the discrete GPU (dGPU) to the integrated GPU (iGPU), so that
the dGPU's VRAM and compute are left entirely to other work such as machine learning,
and moves it back again.
It is meant for desktop PCs with one monitor connected to both GPUs, for example
DisplayPort from the graphics card and HDMI from the motherboard.
.PP
A switch takes effect at the next login, because mutter chooses the GPU it renders
with when GNOME starts.
On a switch,
.B gpu\-vacate
also turns off the output on the other GPU, saves that monitor layout, and points the
monitor at the right input over DDC/CI.
.PP
The mode is kept in
.IR /run ,
so every boot starts in normal mode with the desktop on the dGPU, unless it was vacated
with
.BR "on \-\-keep" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show the mode, the GPU the current GNOME session renders on, and for each GPU its PCI
address and ID, driver, VRAM use and connected outputs.
A GPU that is runtime-suspended is shown as suspended, because reading its VRAM use
would wake it.
This is the default command.
.TP
.BR on " (alias " igpu ") [" \-\-keep ]
Render the desktop on the iGPU, until the next reboot.
With
.B \-\-keep
(or
.BR \-k ),
stay vacated across reboots until
.BR off :
every boot then renders the login screen and the desktop on the iGPU, and switches the
monitor to the iGPU's input before the login screen starts.
Running
.B on
again, with or without
.BR \-\-keep ,
changes whether the mode is kept.
This turns on the iGPU's output, turns off the dGPU's, and switches the monitor's input.
GNOME then asks whether to keep the new display settings: click
.BR "Keep Changes" ,
which is what saves the layout, then press Enter in the terminal.
.B gpu\-vacate
then offers to log out; the next login renders on the iGPU.
It refuses to switch if nothing is connected to the iGPU.
.TP
.BR off " (alias " dgpu )
Render the desktop on the dGPU again, the same way, and forget
.BR \-\-keep .
.TP
.B inputs
For each connected output, show its I2C bus and the input code (DDC/CI VCP feature 0x60)
that the monitor reports.
Run it once with the monitor on each input to find the values for
.B INPUT_DGPU
and
.BR INPUT_IGPU .
Needs
.BR ddcutil (1).
.TP
.B sync
Make the outputs and the monitor input match the current mode, without asking.
Changes made this way last for the session only.
It runs at every GNOME login from
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart/gpu\-vacate\-sync.desktop ,
for example to turn the dGPU's output back on after a reboot from vacated mode.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.TP
.B help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.B restore
Used by
.BR gpu\-vacate\-restore.service ,
which runs it as root at boot, before the login screen starts, if
.B on \-\-keep
was used.
It recreates the flag in
.I /run/gpu\-vacate
for the desktop GPU.
.TP
.BR firmware\-input " " boot | shutdown
Used by
.BR gpu\-vacate\-firmware\-input.service ,
which runs it as root at boot, before the login screen starts, and at shutdown, after it
stops.
It switches the monitor to the boot GPU's input, so the firmware setup, the boot loader
and other operating systems are visible.
At boot after a
.B restore
it switches to the iGPU's input instead.
It does nothing while the desktop is vacated and the display manager is running.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from
.I /etc/gpu\-vacate.conf
by the user command and by the boot and shutdown service.
The file is sourced by
.BR bash (1),
so it uses shell syntax:
.IR NAME = value .
.TP
.B DESKTOP_GPU
The GPU the desktop moves to.
Empty (the default): the integrated GPU, found automatically.
Otherwise a PCI ID
.RI ( vendor : device ,
such as
.BR 1002:164e )
or a PCI address (such as
.BR 0000:5c:00.0 ),
as shown by
.BR "gpu\-vacate status" .
Prefer the ID: addresses can change when a card is added or removed.
.TP
.BR INPUT_DGPU ", " INPUT_IGPU
The monitor's input codes for the cable from each GPU, in hex (such as
.BR 0x0f ).
Empty (the default): the input is left alone, and you switch it from the monitor's menu.
The MCCS standard assigns 0x0f to DisplayPort\-1, 0x10 to DisplayPort\-2, 0x11 to HDMI\-1
and 0x12 to HDMI\-2, but monitors differ; use
.B gpu\-vacate inputs
to read yours.
.TP
.B FIRMWARE_INPUT
.B yes
(the default) to switch the monitor to the boot GPU's input at boot and shutdown, or
.B no
to leave it alone.
Needs the input codes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GPU_VACATE_CONF
Read settings from this file instead of
.IR /etc/gpu\-vacate.conf .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/gpu\-vacate.conf
Settings.
.TP
.IR /run/gpu\-vacate/card N
Exists while the desktop is vacated to card
.IR N .
The directory is recreated empty at every boot and is writable by the
.B gpu\-vacate
group; only its members can switch.
.TP
.I /var/lib/gpu\-vacate/keep
Exists after
.BR "on \-\-keep" ,
until
.BR off .
Writable by the
.B gpu\-vacate
group.
.TP
.I gpu\-vacate\-restore.service
Runs
.B gpu\-vacate restore
at boot when the keep file exists.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/61\-gpu\-vacate.rules
Tags the card named by the flag file with
.BR mutter\-device\-preferred\-primary ,
which makes mutter render on it.
.TP
.I gpu\-vacate\-trigger.path
Re-runs udev on the GPUs whenever the flag directory changes.
.TP
.I gpu\-vacate\-firmware\-input.service
Runs
.B gpu\-vacate firmware\-input
at boot and shutdown.
.TP
.I ~/.config/monitors.xml
GNOME's saved monitor layouts, which
.B on
and
.B off
update.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error or an unknown command.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find the monitor's input codes, then vacate the dGPU:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gpu\-vacate inputs        # monitor showing the dGPU's input
$ gpu\-vacate inputs        # after switching it to the iGPU's input by hand
$ sudoedit /etc/gpu\-vacate.conf
$ gpu\-vacate on
.fi
.RE
.PP
After logging back in, check that the dGPU is idle:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ gpu\-vacate status
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Requires GNOME 48 or newer on Wayland, for
.BR gdctl (1).
Mutter reads its udev tags when GNOME starts, so a switch always needs a log out and
back in.
.PP
Mutter's
.B mutter\-device\-ignore
tag would hide the dGPU completely, but mutter reads it from udev's sticky tag list, so it
cannot be removed again until a reboot.
.B gpu\-vacate
only uses
.BR mutter\-device\-preferred\-primary ,
which mutter reads from the current tags.
.PP
GNOME keeps one saved layout per set of connected monitors.
Adding or removing a GPU renames the connectors, which GNOME treats as a new set, so
settings such as scaling may need to be set again once.
.PP
Some monitors only advertise their native resolution in the DisplayPort EDID.
If the iGPU's HDMI output lacks it, add a mode with a kernel parameter such as
.BR video=HDMI\-A\-3:5120x1440MR@60 .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gdctl (1),
.BR ddcutil (1),
.BR gnome\-shell (1),
.BR udev (7),
.BR systemd.path (5)
.PP
https://github.com/matthewlenz/gpu\-vacate
